The bad rows exist in both `invoices` and `ledger` with identical values, and they were created at t=1 (well within the healthy baseline, `created_at=1`, `synced_at=15`). This means the violation is not new — these rows were always wrong. Let me check whether the invariant checker itself is newly strict, or whether the VAT rounding rule simply doesn't hold for these old rows (i.e. the data was always like this and the signal is a false alarm from a changed check), versus whether invoice-agent has been writing wrong VAT since t=1.

Let me verify the arithmetic on the sample rows and check when the invariant first started firing.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT order_id, amount, vat, total, round(CAST(amount AS REAL) * 0.15, 2) AS expected_vat, round(CAST(amount AS REAL) * 1.15, 2) AS expected_total, created_at FROM invoices WHERE round(CAST(vat AS REAL), 2) != round(CAST(amount AS REAL) * 0.15, 2) OR round(CAST(total AS REAL), 2) != round(CAST(amount AS REAL) + CAST(vat AS REAL), 2) ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 40",
      "why": "Check whether bad invoices span the entire history (created_at from t=1) or only appear after t=517 (the v1.1 deploy), and verify the exact arithmetic mismatch to distinguish a rounding/truncation bug from a wrong rate."
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [],
  "summary": "Bad rows in both invoices and ledger date back to t=1 (baseline), suggesting the VAT calculation has been wrong since the start; need to confirm whether any correctly-calculated rows exist post-deploy to isolate the cause."
}
```
